 systematically, and we haven't yet discussed A, B, D. So we could bring up a candidate to start coverage. Perhaps we should discuss Candidate A's strengths and concerns, compare with C, and maybe adjust recommendation.\n\nWe have to decide our provisional recommendation. We can either stick with Candidate C or propose another. Let's evaluate: Candidate C strong technical, leadership, but concerns about unorganized and not taking criticism. Candidate B strong stress resilience, conscientious, good weather assessment, computer skills, but pretentious and wrong tone. Candidate A quick correct decisions, excellent attention, puts concern for others above everything, but difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education. Candidate D responds to unexpected events, concentrates, solves problems, takes responsibility, but moody, loner.\n\nWhich is best? The criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nCandidate C: technical competence high, leadership high, but concerns on adaptability (feedback) and organization (reliability?). Not sure about stress resilience. No info. Crew cooperation? Leadership suggests maybe good, but unfriendly and eats unhealthily (unfriendly may affect crew cooperation). So crew cooperation may be weak.\n\nCandidate B: stress resilience high, conscientious (reliability), good technical (weather, computer), but pretentious and wrong tone (crew cooperation, communication). So crew cooperation moderate.\n\nCandidate A: quick correct decisions (decision quality), excellent attention (attention), puts concern for others (crew cooperation), but difficulty communicating, egocentric (crew cooperation, communication), not willing to further education (adaptability). Reliability unknown but maybe okay.\n\nCandidate D: problem solving, responsibility, concentration, responds to unexpected events (stress resilience), but moody, loner (crew cooperation), considered moody (reliability?), maybe less.\n\nThus overall, Candidate B seems balanced: reliability (conscientious), stress resilience, technical competence, but communication issues. Candidate A has strong decision and crew concern but communication issues. Candidate C strong technical but feedback issues. Candidate D strong responsibility but interpersonal issues.\n\nGiven the need for crew cooperation and communication, maybe Candidate B's issues are more severe (pretentious, wrong tone) could affect safety. Candidate A's difficulty communicating ideas also serious. Candidate C's unfriendly also problematic.\n\nWe need to decide a provisional recommendation. Perhaps we can suggest Candidate B as a strong alternative, but we need to bring up evidence. Or we could keep Candidate C but note concerns and ask for more info on stress resilience and crew cooperation for C, and also bring up other candidates.\n\nWe should follow the rule: each turn should be focused, referencing discussion. Since no prior public statements, we can start by presenting Candidate A's profile and compare to C, maybe argue that A's crew cooperation (puts concern for others) outweighs C's unfriendly. But we need to decide vote.\n\nMaybe we can propose Candidate B as provisional recommendation, providing evidence: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), but note concerns: pretentious, wrong tone (crew cooperation, communication). Compare to C's technical strengths but feedback issues.\n\nThus we can argue B is more balanced.\n\nLet's do that: Provide a concise argument, mention facts, and vote for Candidate B.\n\nWe must include METADATA_JSON with vote.\n\nProceed."}
